}]},{"id":"GGIS 392","title":"Geography & GIS Internship","creditHours":"0 TO 3 hours.","sections":[{"crn":"74218","code":"","type":"Independent Study","partOfTerm":"1","meetings":[{"type":"Independent Study","days":[],"start":null,"end":null,"location":"Location Pending","instructors":[]}],"restrictions":[],"note":""}]},{"id":"GGIS 395","title":"Topics in Geography & GIS","creditHours":"3 hours.","sections":[{"crn":"79085","code":"JM","type":"Lecture-Discussion","partOfTerm":"1","meetings":[{"type":"Lecture-Discussion","days":["T"],"start":840,"end":1010,"location":"Location Pending","instructors":["McAlister, J"]}],"restrictions":[],"note":"This course explores the historical and material landscapes of the United States. Students will learn about regional architecture styles and spread, historical and cultural influences, and the various impacts that geography has had on the built environment of the country. Key topics include migration patterns, observing the built landscape, and historic preservation. Students will develop the skills needed to understand and work with the National Register of Historic Places and state and local historic preservation offices."},{"crn":"78853","code":"SZ","type":"Lecture-Discussion","partOfTerm":"1","meetings":[{"type":"Lecture-Discussion","days":["M","W"],"start":630,"end":710,"location":"2084 Natural History Building","instructors":["Zaragocin, S"]}],"restrictions":[],"note":"This course examines the dialectical relationship between gender and the social construction of space. Students will engage with key themes in feminist geography, including gender, space and place, embodiment and everyday life, and intersectionality. Moving across multiple scales—from the body to the world system—we will explore how gender, sexuality, race, class, and coloniality shape spatial relations in diverse contexts. The course draws on feminist geographic scholarship from both the Global North and the Global South, emphasizing how feminist methodologies—such as reflexivity, translation, participatory research, social cartography, and embodied methods—can produce more equitable and transformative understandings of geography."}]},{"id":"GGIS 403","title":"Geographic Information Science and Systems","creditHours":"4 hours.","sections":[{"crn":"74455","code":"MS","type":"Online","partOfTerm":"A","meetings":[{"type":"Online","days":[],"start":null,"end":null,"location":"","instructors":["Portelli, R"]}],"restrictions":["Restricted to NDEG:WeatherClimteRA ONL-UIUC, MS:WeatherClimteRA ONL-UIUC, MS:Environ Geology ONL-UIUC, NDEG:Environ Geology ONL-UIUC, MS:GIS & Data Sci ONL - UIUC, or NDEG:GIS & Data Sci ONL -UIUC."],"note":""},{"crn":"74453","code":"ONL","type":"Online","partOfTerm":"A","meetings":[{"type":"Online","days":[],"start":null,"end":null,"location":"","instructors":["Portelli, R"]}],"restrictions":["Not intended for NDEG:WeatherClimteRA ONL-UIUC, MS:WeatherClimteRA ONL-UIUC, MS:Environ Geology ONL-UIUC, NDEG:Environ Geology ONL-UIUC, MS:GIS & Data Sci ONL - UIUC, or NDEG:GIS & Data Sci ONL -UIUC."],"note":""}]},{"id":"GGIS 407","title":"CyberGIS & Geospatial Data Science","creditHours":"4 hours.","sections":[{"crn":"74456","code":"MS","type":"Online","partOfTerm":"B","meetings":[{"type":"Online","days":[],"start":null,"end":null,"location":"","instructors":["Padmanabhan, A"]}],"restrictions":["Restricted to NDEG:WeatherClimteRA ONL-UIUC, MS:WeatherClimteRA ONL-UIUC, MS:Environ Geology ONL-UIUC, NDEG:Environ Geology ONL-UIUC, MS:GIS & Data Sci ONL - UIUC, or NDEG:GIS & Data Sci ONL -UIUC."],"note":""},{"crn":"74454","code":"ONL","type":"Online","partOfTerm":"B","meetings":[{"type":"Online","days":[],"start":null,"end":null,"location":"","instructors":["Padmanabhan, A"]}],"restrictions":["Not intended for NDEG:WeatherClimteRA ONL-UIUC, MS:WeatherClimteRA ONL-UIUC, MS:Environ Geology ONL-UIUC, NDEG:Environ Geology ONL-UIUC, MS:GIS & Data Sci ONL - UIUC, or NDEG:GIS & Data Sci ONL -UIUC."],"note":""}]},{"id"